<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdalonymus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdalonymus was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 332 BCE. Ancient writers say he was a poor gardener of royal descent chosen in place of the deposed king Straton. The Alexander Sarcophagus, found at Sidon and now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, is thought to have been made for him, and he ruled as a Macedonian client king.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdalonymus.jpg" alt="Abdalonymus" width="320" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marsyas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Achaemenid_cavalry_on_Alexander_Sarcophagus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abdalonymus, whose Phoenician name means servant of the highest gods, was of royal descent but had fallen so far into poverty that he lived by cultivating a kitchen garden. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> had subdued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, he allowed his companion Hephaestion to give its crown to whomever he pleased. Hephaestion offered it to two brothers in whose house he lodged, but they refused, saying that by their laws only someone of royal blood could wear it, and named Abdalonymus instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Sent by Hephaestion with the crown and royal robes, the brothers found Abdalonymus weeding in his garden. They had him wash, dressed him in the emblems of royalty and brought him to Alexander, who asked how he had borne his poverty. Abdalonymus is said to have answered that he hoped he could bear his prosperity as well, since his own hands had met all his needs and, having nothing, he had wanted nothing. Pleased with the reply, Alexander confirmed the choice, gave the new king the palace and private estate of his predecessor Strato, and added lands from the neighbouring country to his realm.</p><p>The story comes from Quintus Curtius and is confirmed by Justin, but other writers tell it differently. Diodorus calls the man Ballonimus and makes him king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> moves the scene to Paphos and names him Alonymus. Curtius probably decorated the story with invented details.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Alexander Sarcophagus, found near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> and now in the Istanbul Archaeological Museum, is generally thought to be his. Its carving shows him hunting beside Alexander. Some scholars, however, believe the sarcophagus belonged instead to Mazaeus, a Persian noble and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abdalonymus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Hellenistic necropolis has yielded the Alexander Sarcophagus, likely the tomb of King <b>Abdalonymus</b> rather than of Alexander, the Lycian tomb and the Sarcophagus of the Crying Women, all now in the Archaeological Museum of Istanbul.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.56,35.38&amp;year=-335" aria-label="Sidon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Abdalonymus"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5382 4687h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5382 4687h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.56,35.38&amp;year=-335">What was here in 335 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
